    <summary>You describe your services once. Enodia handles the rest.</summary>
    <description># Enodia (Portable)
## What it does

```
Know what you are running, and how long it has left.

          ░░
         ▒██▒
        ▓████▓
  ▓    ▓██████▓    ▓
 ▓█▓░  ▒██████▒  ░▓█▓
 ██████▓▒▓██▓▒▓██████
 ▒███████▓▒▒▓███████▒
  ░▒▓█████░░█████▓▒░
        ▒████▒
          ██
          ▒▒

█▀▀ █▄ █ █▀█ █▀▄ █ ▄▀█
██▄ █ ▀█ █▄█ █▄▀ █ █▀█ asks your deployed services what version they are running, then checks

those versions against vendor lifecycle calendars and tells you what is
already dead, what is dying, and where your fleet has drifted apart.

You describe your services once. Enodia handles the rest.
```

    </description>
    <releaseNotes>## [2.1.1+0] — 2026-10-08

### Fixed

- MariaDB 11.0+ no longer masks its version behind `5.5.5-` (`11.4.9-MariaDB-…`),
  so `mysql` recorded such servers as MySQL and `mariadb` refused them. Both
  probes now recognise MariaDB by either shape (D47).
    </releaseNotes>
